00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:09.189
And let's get into it.

00:01:09.189 --> 00:01:16.170
It’s a fact, China is hot right now.

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:20.250
The Beijing regime’s influence over the
world has never been as great as it is today,

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:24.009
and the same applies to China's fame and presence
in global media.

00:01:24.009 --> 00:01:25.009
And, you know what?

00:01:25.009 --> 00:01:32.399
The person responsible for all this is this
man.

00:01:32.399 --> 00:01:41.570
Xi Jinping has become the Chinese president
with the most power since the fall of Mao

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:42.570
Zedong.

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:47.820
He came to power in 2013 with two great promises:
on the one hand, to expand China's power and

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:52.590
influence over the world, and on the other,
to consolidate the communist party’s political

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:53.590
power.

00:01:53.590 --> 00:01:57.590
That way, the Chinese president seeks to end
the widespread belief that economic development

00:01:57.590 --> 00:02:02.329
is always followed by political transitions
and democratic processes.

00:02:02.329 --> 00:02:09.990
But folks, to achieve his goals and consolidate
his power, Xi Jinping first needs to remove

00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:15.890
two large pebbles from his shoe, two pebbles
he perceives as a threat to China’s cohesion

00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:17.490
and national identity:

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:21.860
I’m talking about Taiwan and Hong Kong.

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:38.440
And it’s precisely the second one, Hong
Kong, which we’ll talk about in this video.

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:43.160
As you probably all know, Hong Kong is currently
experiencing the greatest civil protests since

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:48.200
the end of British colonization, and they
might also become the longest protests.

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:50.410
The trigger?: The Extradition Law.

00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:56.820
You see, this law proposed by Hong Kong’s
Chief Executive, Carrie Lam, following Beijing’s

00:02:56.820 --> 00:03:01.200
directives, would allow for the extradition
of Hong Kong citizens to be tried and, if

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:03.230
applicable, imprisoned in mainland China.

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:07.730
In other words, this proposed Law would recognize
the validity of the courts controlled by the

00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:09.060
Chinese Communist Party.

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:13.460
And even though in the end, after pressure
from the protesters, the proposed Law was

00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:18.650
withdrawn, it’s a clear example of how things
are working in Hong Kong.

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:24.090
But why was this particular proposal the last
straw for the people of Hong Kong?

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:26.090
What does Carrie Lam's proposal mean?

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:30.440
Are we witnessing a historical turning point
in the relationship between the former colony

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:33.170
and China?

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:37.640
Listen up.

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:44.010
(THE EXTRADITION LAW, THE STRAW THAT BROKE
THE CAMEL’S BACK)

00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:47.880
That's right, the Extradition Law is the proverbial
straw that broke the camel’s back.

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:51.660
Because... don’t think it all began with
this legislation, not at all.

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:55.960
Beijing's interference in Hong Kong politics
and the promotion of pro-Chinese laws by Hong

00:03:55.960 --> 00:03:59.930
Kong authorities – which actually emanate
from the continental regime itself, from the

00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:03.950
Chinese regime itself – have been somewhat
constant since the United Kingdom transferred

00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:05.990
Hong Kong back to China in 1997.

00:04:05.990 --> 00:04:07.240
Want an example?

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:11.460
Well, check this out.

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:13.430
Let's go back to the year 2003.

00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:17.630
Just six years after its transfer to China,
the Hong Kong executive proposed the so-called

00:04:17.630 --> 00:04:21.750
National Security Law, which sought to ban
and punish pro-independence movements.

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:26.090
Beijing was seeking, and I say seeking because
the Law was repealed, to shield its sovereignty

00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:29.090
over the territory and stamp out any scrap
of opposition.

00:04:29.090 --> 00:04:33.220
However, it didn’t let a small obstacle
get in its way, China ran into fierce opposition

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:37.070
from Hong Kong’s citizens, who took to the
streets, leading to the greatest protests

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:38.730
in Hong Kong’s history...

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:40.090
Until now....

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:46.960
Yes, folks, because the protests of this year,
2019, have already far exceeded those of 2003.

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:54.760
And the truth is that there are plenty of
reasons why.

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:58.170
Imagine that you live in a territory with
an independent economic system, guaranteed

00:04:58.170 --> 00:05:02.700
justice and a society with a lot of freedoms
such as, free access to the internet and social

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:03.700
networks.

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:07.910
And, suddenly, a dictatorial regime with which
you don’t identify at all, a regime where

00:05:07.910 --> 00:05:12.420
none of these rights and freedoms exist and
where justice is controlled by a single party,

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:16.690
seeks to impose a Law that would allow them
to extradite you to be judged under that regime

00:05:16.690 --> 00:05:22.680
with its conditions, what would you do?

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:26.500
Discontent and anger seem more than justified,
don't you think?

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:30.140
After all, the extradition law, even though
it apparently doesn’t specifically mention

00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:34.240
political opposition,, marks the end of Hong
Kong's model and autonomy.

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:35.400
Because of course...

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:39.590
Don’t tell me it’s hard to imagine a Chinese
court asking for the extradition of a dissident

00:05:39.590 --> 00:05:45.740
or a political opponent from Hong Kong for
any reason.

00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:49.460
Right?

00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:53.900
( Hong Kong extradition law is a death sentence
for Hong Kong, says exiled bookseller Lam

00:05:53.900 --> 00:05:54.900
Wing-kee.

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:58.570
“Beijing will use this law to control Hong
Kong completely.

00:05:58.570 --> 00:06:00.240
Freedom of speech will be lost.

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:04.130
In the past, the regime kidnapped its critics,
like me, illegally.

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:07.540
With this law, they will abduct their critics
legally.”

00:06:07.540 --> 00:06:08.540
Hong Kong Free Press)

00:06:08.540 --> 00:06:11.720
Well, the fact is that the discontent is so
great that according to a recent study of

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:15.520
the Public Opinion Program of the University
of Hong Kong, rejection of China had never

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:21.140
been as strong among Hong Kong citizens as
it is now.

00:06:21.140 --> 00:06:32.560
But that’s not all.

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:36.580
The worst is the general feeling that the
Hong Kong executive responds to China’s

00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:40.480
interests and not those of its own local citizens.

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:47.210
And that’s a problem in a territory where
citizens cannot change things because the

00:06:47.210 --> 00:06:49.660
truth is that democracy doesn’t exist.

00:06:49.660 --> 00:06:53.140
In Hong Kong not all citizens are allowed
to vote and the government chief executive

00:06:53.140 --> 00:06:55.280
himself is proposed by Beijing.

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:59.380
You see, in a normal, democratic and free
country, if a ruler does badly, if he or she

00:06:59.380 --> 00:07:03.870
rules against the people’s interests or
is discovered favoring other interests, people

00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:11.590
stop voting for that person or that party,
and then they can start undoing the bad policies.

00:07:11.590 --> 00:07:16.210
However, that can’t happen here.

00:07:16.210 --> 00:07:20.370
After transferring the territory’s sovereignty
to China, the political system turned into

00:07:20.370 --> 00:07:25.650
something very, very similar to a corporatist
regime that governs for the interests of two

00:07:25.650 --> 00:07:26.870
powerful hands:

00:07:26.870 --> 00:07:31.560
On the one hand that of the Chinese iron fist
and on the other, the money-filled hands of

00:07:31.560 --> 00:07:35.710
the great businessmen that favor Beijing;
especially those of the great real estate

00:07:35.710 --> 00:07:40.120
tycoons that have managed to form an oligopoly
where Hong Kong is nothing more than a captive

00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:43.169
market that acts at their service.

00:07:43.169 --> 00:07:44.169
Surprised?

00:07:44.169 --> 00:07:47.400
Well… you shouldn’t be.

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:54.770
In fact, the Hong Kong political system feeds
this model, a lot.

00:07:54.770 --> 00:08:01.310
In fact, we’ve already talked about this
in a previous video where we tell you why

00:08:01.310 --> 00:08:04.030
Hong Kong has the most expensive housing in
the world.

00:08:04.030 --> 00:08:07.560
The point is that in Hong Kong, the Chief
Executive – the governmental leader – is

00:08:07.560 --> 00:08:11.880
elected by the members of the Election Committee,
which has about 1,200 people.

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:14.030
And... who chooses those 1,200 representatives?

00:08:14.030 --> 00:08:19.480
Well, to be honest, it’s easier to start
by counting those who don’t vote: the citizens.

00:08:19.480 --> 00:08:23.840
We’re talking about a very complicated system
in which sectoral organizations, religious

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:27.230
leaders, business leaders, unions and even
companies participate.

00:08:27.230 --> 00:08:30.870
All orchestrated to endorse China’s power.

00:08:30.870 --> 00:08:35.770
For example, check out what’s happening
with the agriculture sector, which is traditionally

00:08:35.770 --> 00:08:37.140
connected to Beijing.

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:41.900
Despite only involving 0.1% of Hong Kong’s
economy, farmers hold more weight in the Election

00:08:41.900 --> 00:08:46.440
Committee than much, much more important sectors
such as the financial sector.

00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:51.890
As you can see, it’s all very democratic,
right?.

00:08:51.890 --> 00:08:56.990
In fact, the lack of democracy is the real
reason behind the current protests and behind

00:08:56.990 --> 00:08:58.490
many past protests.

00:08:58.490 --> 00:09:02.950
Because, in spite of the fact that democracy
doesn’t technically exist, Hong Kong is

00:09:02.950 --> 00:09:13.830
and has been a regional epicenter of political
activism and dissent against Beijing for decades.

00:09:13.830 --> 00:09:18.520
And the Law of Extradition was the last straw
in a process that is spelling the end of all

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:19.520
those freedoms.

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:21.900
If you think about it, it’s the most dramatic
part of it.

00:09:21.900 --> 00:09:25.820
In Hong Kong we aren’t facing a dictatorship
that wants to get its way, we’re witnessing

00:09:25.820 --> 00:09:30.440
people who are used to living in freedom,
and who are watching that freedom slip away

00:09:30.440 --> 00:09:31.440
every day.

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:37.570
And that explains what comes next, listen
up.

00:09:37.570 --> 00:09:43.750
(THE TRIPLE FREEDOM, DOWNHILL WITH NO BRAKES)

00:09:43.750 --> 00:09:47.060
That's right folks, in Hong Kong the three
freedoms are in free fall.

00:09:47.060 --> 00:09:51.070
Economic, individual and political freedom,
all three, have worsened since China took

00:09:51.070 --> 00:09:53.520
over in 1997.

00:09:53.520 --> 00:10:00.850
And that has consequences on every level.

00:10:00.850 --> 00:10:04.990
Especially in a place that has been traditionally
known for its scrupulous respect for legal

00:10:04.990 --> 00:10:07.110
certainty and institutional stability.

00:10:07.110 --> 00:10:12.630
And that’s precisely why around 1,500 multinational
companies have their Asian headquarters in

00:10:12.630 --> 00:10:13.630
Hong Kong.

00:10:13.630 --> 00:10:15.430
However, folks, all this may end badly.

00:10:15.430 --> 00:10:20.620
Not only in the political field, but also
in the economic field.

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:27.240
China’s continuous interference is, in many
cases, translating into serious, very serious

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:29.900
violations of several companies’ legal safety.

00:10:29.900 --> 00:10:34.190
In the economic field, China has increased
its blackmailing of private companies so they’ll

00:10:34.190 --> 00:10:38.010
maintain a critical stance against any movement
that opposes Beijing.

00:10:38.010 --> 00:10:41.950
That is, if they don't want to have any trouble
staying in the big Chinese market.

00:10:41.950 --> 00:10:46.770
That’s what happened, for example, with
the Hong Kong airline Cathay Pacific, which

00:10:46.770 --> 00:10:50.891
suffered the consequences of not being “critical”
during the protests against the Extradition

00:10:50.891 --> 00:10:56.130
Lawand not sufficiently controlling its workers’
political expressions.

00:10:56.130 --> 00:11:02.350
(Cathay Pacific replaces chief executive as
China ups pressure over protests.

00:11:02.350 --> 00:11:03.350
Financial Times )

00:11:03.350 --> 00:11:06.280
(Cathay Pacific staff speak of climate of
fear.

00:11:06.280 --> 00:11:07.280
BBC)

00:11:07.280 --> 00:11:11.960
(Cathay Pacific employees say they fear losing
their jobs if they express support for pro-democracy

00:11:11.960 --> 00:11:13.910
protesters in Hong Kong.

00:11:13.910 --> 00:11:19.060
Some are considering bringing decoy mobiles
with them in case their phones are checked

00:11:19.060 --> 00:11:20.870
on arrival in the mainland.)

00:11:20.870 --> 00:11:24.630
But no, it doesn’t end there.

00:11:24.630 --> 00:11:29.050
Folks, the repression of the business sector,
of the productive sectors, is even reaching

00:11:29.050 --> 00:11:33.370
cases of boycotts, organized by the Chinese
government itself through social networks.

00:11:33.370 --> 00:11:36.580
And nobody’s safe, not even bubble tea.

00:11:36.580 --> 00:11:42.820
(Bubble tea brawl: Taiwan brands face mainland
boycott over Hong Kong gesture.

00:11:42.820 --> 00:11:43.820
Reuters)

00:11:43.820 --> 00:11:46.680
Worst of all, we aren’t even talking about
small isolated cases.

00:11:46.680 --> 00:11:48.850
Not at all.

00:11:48.850 --> 00:11:52.990
Xi Jinping knows that he runs a country that
has one of the most desired markets for any

00:11:52.990 --> 00:11:54.220
company worth its salt.

00:11:54.220 --> 00:11:58.660
An overwhelmingly huge population, a growing
middle class and consumer power that is already

00:11:58.660 --> 00:12:00.040
at exorbitant levels.

00:12:00.040 --> 00:12:04.750
To be in business in China is to be close
to success, and Xi knows that this is something

00:12:04.750 --> 00:12:11.070
he can leverage to politically press people,
by using commerce and the business sector.

00:12:11.070 --> 00:12:13.640
And that obviously doesn’t reassure any
company.

00:12:13.640 --> 00:12:14.990
One misstep... and...

00:12:14.990 --> 00:12:17.649
Also, how on earth are they going to control
their employees?

00:12:17.649 --> 00:12:20.130
How can they limit the freedom of expression
of others?

00:12:20.130 --> 00:12:23.380
How can they attract international talent
with such conditions?

00:12:23.380 --> 00:12:28.410
Anyway, it starts with bubble tea, and ends
with any company that doesn’t do what China

00:12:28.410 --> 00:12:35.640
wants, and of course, this isn’t just reprehensible,
it’s irresponsible.

00:12:35.640 --> 00:12:40.040
China’s attitude, along with the blatant
protection of local oligarchs and the submission

00:12:40.040 --> 00:12:43.020
of the local government are ending Hong Kong's
prosperity.

00:12:43.020 --> 00:12:46.649
The people of Hong Kong feel like they’re
not only losing their political freedoms,

00:12:46.649 --> 00:12:48.770
but also their own economic well-being.

00:12:48.770 --> 00:12:53.070
Because if Hong Kong loses its independence,
legal certainty and neutrality, what’s left?

00:12:53.070 --> 00:13:01.800
Folks, the worst part is that Hong Kong may
simply stop being Hong Kong.

00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:05.410
Surely now you may better understand why the
protests happened and why they’re lasting

00:13:05.410 --> 00:13:06.410
so long.

00:13:06.410 --> 00:13:09.959
Hong Kong’s society feels like it’s facing
one of its last chances to fight back.

00:13:09.959 --> 00:13:11.110
It’s now or never.

00:13:11.110 --> 00:13:15.081
But if you want to know why Xi Jinping is
so interested in Hong Kong, and why, despite

00:13:15.081 --> 00:13:19.271
all of this, China still needs this small
territory, stay tuned because we’ll bring

00:13:19.271 --> 00:13:23.080
more videos about Hong Kong in which we’ll
explain these and many other things.

00:13:23.080 --> 00:13:27.050
But now it's your turn, do you think that
the pressure of the people of Hong Kong will

00:13:27.050 --> 00:13:29.850
help them regain their political and individual
freedoms?

00:13:29.850 --> 00:13:32.820
Will China be able to squash Hong Kong's languishing
political freedom?

00:13:32.820 --> 00:13:36.120
Is this the beginning of the end of Hong Kong
as we know it?

